  "translationRepairs": [
    ["s0087","literal","The emperor said: \"Rites value hitting the mean; excess is extravagance, shortfall is austerity—the previous dynasty judged this with precision.","The emperor said: \"Rites value hitting the mean; excess is extravagance, shortfall is austerity—the previous court judged this with precision.","Yongle is speaking within the Ming; 先朝 refers to the preceding Hongwu court/reign, not another dynasty, so 'previous court' is the accurate literal.","high","applied"],
    ["s0089","literal","Since the Nine Dragons carriage did not exist in the previous dynasty, let things remain as they are.\"","Since the Nine Dragons carriage did not exist under the previous court, let things remain as they are.\"","Same 先朝 as in s0087: the Nine Dragons carriage was absent under the previous Ming court, not under a prior dynasty; 'previous court' restores the sense.","high","applied"],
    ["s0091","idiomatic","Duty commandants each wore goose-down caps, single-layer grandson garments, copper belts, and full sets of boots and shoes.","Duty commandants each wore goose-down caps, jisun garments, copper belts, and full sets of boots and shoes.","只孫衣 is the Yuan–Ming ceremonial jisun robe (a loanword garment name), not 'single-layer grandson garments'; the proposed 'jisun garments' corrects the mistranslation.","high","applied"],
    ["s0091","literal","The guard commandants on duty each wore goose-down caps, single-layer grandson garments, copper belts, and one set each of boots, shoes, and sandals.","The guard commandants on duty each wore goose-down caps, jisun garments, copper belts, and one set each of boots, shoes, and sandals.","Literal likewise misread 只孫 as 'grandson'; replacing with 'jisun garments' matches the ceremonial robe term in the Chinese.","high","applied"],
    ["s0111","idiomatic","The heir apparent's ceremonial train followed the same rules.","The ceremonial train of a prince's heir followed the same rules.","Following 親王儀仗, 世子 is a prince's heir, not the heir apparent (皇太子); the proposal's sense is right, but stacked 'prince's heir's' is awkward, so rephrase.","high","applied"],
    ["s0111","literal","The heir apparent's ceremonial regalia was the same in all respects.","The ceremonial regalia of a prince's heir was the same in all respects.","世子 after 親王 is a prince's heir, not the crown prince; keep that correction but avoid the awkward double possessive in the proposal.","high","applied"],
    ["s0117","idiomatic","Imperial princesses and heirs' consorts followed the same rules.","Imperial princesses and consorts of princes' heirs followed the same rules.","After 親王妃 and distinct from 東宮妃 in s0115, 世子妃 are consorts of princes' heirs; the proposed wording correctly separates them from the heir apparent.","high","applied"],
    ["s0117","literal","Imperial princesses and consorts of heirs apparent had the same ceremonial regalia.","Imperial princesses and consorts of princes' heirs had the same ceremonial regalia.","世子妃 means consorts of 世子 (princes' heirs), not of heirs apparent; the literal repair matches the Chinese and the surrounding rank sequence.","high","applied"]
  ],
